  "reactivity-series": {
    "type": "process",
    "title": "Overview of Reactivity Series & Displacement",
    "items": [
      {
        "label": "Reactivity Series & Displacement",
        "content": "Key concept related to Reactivity Series & Displacement",
        "color": "#10b981"
      },
      {
        "label": "1\\. Definition and Scope",
        "content": "The reactivity series (also called the activity series) is an ordered list of metals (and hydrogen) based on their tendency to lose electrons and form cations. It is a practical manifestation of the standard electrode potentials (E°) measured under standard conditions (298 K, 1 atm, 1 M). The series is indispensable for predicting the direction of displacement reactions, corrosion behaviour, and the feasibility of metal extraction processes.",
        "color": "#3b82f6"
      },
      {
        "label": "2\\. Construction of the Reactivity Series",
        "content": "Electrochemical Measurements: Laboratory determination of E° values using a galvanic cell with the metal as one electrode and the standard hydrogen electrode (SHE) as the reference.",
        "color": "#f59e0b"
      },
      {
        "label": "3\\. Standard Electrode Potentials (_E°_) – Representative Values",
        "content": "<table style=\"width:100%; bordercollapse:collapse; margintop:12px;\"><thead><tr style=\"background:#2a2a3a;\"><th style=\"border:1px solid #555;padding:8px;\"><strong>Metal</strong></th><th style=\"border:1px solid #555;padding:8px;\"><strong>E° (V)</strong> (vs. SHE)</th><th style=\"border:1px solid #555;padding:8px;\"><strong>Position in Series</strong></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr style=\"background:#1e1e2b;\"><td style=\"border:1px solid #555;padding:8px;\"><strong>[[Potassium|K]]</strong></td><td style=\"border:1px solid #555;padding:8px;\">2.93</td><td style=\"border:1px solid #555;padding:8px;\">Most reactive (top)</td></tr><tr><td style=\"border:1px solid #555;padding:8px;\"><strong>[[Sodium|Na]]</strong></td><td style=\"border:1px solid #555;padding:8px;\">2.71</td><td style=\"border:1px solid #555;padding:8px;\">Alkali metal</td></tr><tr style=\"background:#1e1e2b;\"><td style=\"border:1px solid #555;padding:8px;\"><strong>[[Calcium|Ca]]</strong></td><td style=\"border:1px solid #555;padding:8px;\">2.87</td><td style=\"border:1px solid #555;padding:8px;\">Alkaline earth</td></tr><tr><td style=\"border:1px solid #555;padding:8px;\"><strong>[[Magnesium|Mg]]</strong></td><td style=\"border:1px solid #555;padding:8px;\">2.37</td><td style=\"border:1px solid #555;padding:8px;\">Alkaline earth</td></tr><tr style=\"background:#1e1e2b;\"><td style=\"border:1px solid #555;padding:8px;\"><strong>[[Aluminium|Al]]</strong></td><td style=\"border:1px solid #555;padding:8px;\">1.66</td><td style=\"border:1px solid #555;padding:8px;\">Post‑transition</td></tr><tr><td style=\"border:1px solid #555;padding:8px;\"><strong>[[Zinc|Zn]]</strong></td><td style=\"border:1px solid #555;padding:8px;\">0.76</td><td style=\"border:1px solid #555;padding:8px;\">Transition metal</td></tr><tr style=\"background:#1e1e2b;\"><td style=\"border:1px solid #555;padding:8px;\"><strong>[[Iron|Fe]]</strong></td><td style=\"border:1px solid #555;padding:8px;\">0.44</td><td style=\"border:1px solid #555;padding:8px;\">Transition metal</td></tr><tr><td style=\"border:1px solid #555;padding:8px;\"><strong>[[Nickel|Ni]]</strong></td><td style=\"border:1px solid #555;padding:8px;\">0.25</td><td style=\"border:1px solid #555;padding:8px;\">Transition metal</td></tr><tr style=\"background:#1e1e2b;\"><td style=\"border:1px solid #555;padding:8px;\"><strong>[[Copper|Cu]]</strong></td><td style=\"border:1px solid #555;padding:8px;\">+0.34</td><td style=\"border:1px solid #555;padding:8px;\">Less reactive</td></tr><tr><td style=\"border:1px solid #555;padding:8px;\"><strong>[[Silver|Ag]]</strong></td><td style=\"border:1px solid #555;padding:8px;\">+0.80</td><td style=\"border:1px solid #555;padding:8px;\">Low reactivity</td></tr><tr style=\"background:#1e1e2b;\"><td style=\"border:1px solid #555;padding:8px;\"><strong>[[Gold|Au]]</strong></td><td style=\"border:1px solid #555;padding:8px;\">+1.50</td><td style=\"border:1px solid #555;padding:8px;\">Least reactive (bottom)</td></tr><tr><td style=\"border:1px solid #555;padding:8px;\"><strong>[[Hydrogen|H]]</strong></td><td style=\"border:1px solid #555;padding:8px;\">0.00</td><td style=\"border:1px solid #555;padding:8px;\">Reference point</td></tr></tbody></table>",
        "color": "#ef4444"
      },
      {
        "label": "4\\. Fundamental Principles Governing the Series",
        "content": "Ionisation Energy: Metals with low first ionisation energies release electrons more readily, moving them upward in the series.",
        "color": "#8b5cf6"
      },
      {
        "label": "5\\. Types of Displacement Reactions",
        "content": "Displacement reactions are redox processes where a more reactive metal replaces a less reactive one from its compound. They are classified as:",
        "color": "#06b6d4"
      },
      {
        "label": "6\\. Predicting Reaction Feasibility Using the Series",
        "content": "To decide whether a displacement will occur, compare the positions of the reacting metal and the metal ion in the series:",
        "color": "#eab308"
      },
      {
        "label": "7\\. Industrial Applications of Reactivity Series",
        "content": "Thermal reduction of Fe₂O₃ using carbon (coke) exploits iron’s lower position than carbon in the series.",
        "color": "#f97316"
      }
    ]
  },
